\1\n    <div id="site-header"></div>
    <!-- Header -->
    
    <main class="lesson-container py-4">
        <!-- Breadcrumb -->
        <div id="tutorial-header"></div>
        
        <div class="content lesson-content" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary);">

            <p>A <strong>Site-to-Site VPN</strong> creates a secure, encrypted tunnel between two distinct geographic networks across an untrusted network like the Internet.</p>
            
            <h3>The Conceptual Model</h3>
            <p>Imagine an organization with two physical locations:</p>
            <pre><code>Office A Subnet: 192.168.10.0/24
Office B Subnet: 192.168.20.0/24

Office A Gateway → Internet (Encrypted Tunnel) ← Office B Gateway</code></pre>
            <p>Devices in Office A can communicate with devices in Office B using their private IP addresses. The VPN gateways at the edge of each network handle the encryption and routing invisibly to the end users.</p>
            
            <h3>IPsec and IKE Concepts</h3>
            <p>Most enterprise site-to-site VPNs utilize IPsec (Internet Protocol Security). Building the tunnel generally involves two conceptual phases:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>IKE Phase 1 / IKE SA:</strong> The two VPN gateways authenticate each other and negotiate a secure management channel.</li>
                <li><strong>IPsec / Child SA (Phase 2):</strong> Within the secure Phase 1 channel, the gateways negotiate the actual encryption parameters and establish the data tunnel for the specific subnets (the "interesting traffic" or selectors).</li>
            </ul>
            <p><em>Note: Be careful, as IKEv1 and IKEv2 terminology differs. Do not imply "Phase 2" terminology applies identically to every modern IKEv2 implementation, where they are often referred to as Child SAs.</em></p>
            
            <h3>Common Troubleshooting Problems</h3>
            <p>If a Site-to-Site VPN fails to establish or pass traffic, check for these common issues:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Mismatched Proposals:</strong> Encryption algorithms, hash algorithms, or Diffie-Hellman groups do not match on both sides.</li>
                <li><strong>Incorrect Subnets (Selectors):</strong> The defined local and remote subnets do not perfectly mirror each other.</li>
                <li><strong>Routing and NAT:</strong> The gateway doesn't know to route the traffic into the tunnel, or NAT is incorrectly translating the private IPs before they enter the tunnel (NAT Exemption is often required).</li>
                <li><strong>Firewall Rules:</strong> Ensure UDP ports 500 and 4500 (for NAT-T) are permitted on the external interfaces, and the decrypted inner traffic is permitted by the <a href="stateful-firewall-explained.html">stateful firewall</a> policies.</li>
            </ul>
        
        </div>
        
